(project pricklypear-formal-verification
 (doc "Pricklypear book chapter: formal verification of the OCaml outer shell")
 (status planned)
 (convention ocaml-dune)
 (section formal-verification
  (doc "Machine-checked stability for the pure OCaml core; Lisp garden may stay janky")
  (status planned)

  (subsection goals
   (doc "What formal verification means here, and what it does not")
   (status planned)
   (details
    (doc (|
         MOTIVATION (from the book roadmap):
         Lisp is the LLM/authoring surface and may stay a little
         janky. The OCaml outer shell — interpreter kernel, effect
         lattice, auth primitives, store decision logic — should
         become incredibly stable. Formal verification is how we
         earn that stability without freezing product velocity.

         DEFINITION IN THIS PROJECT:
         Formal verification = stating invariants as named
         properties, then checking them by increasingly strong
         methods:
           1. property-based tests (QCheck)
           2. exhaustive/model checks on small finite domains
           3. machine-checked proofs (Gospel/Cameleer, Why3, later Coq)
         A property is not "verified" merely because an example
         alcotest passes.

         RELATION TO BORGE:
         Borge remains the intent map and drift tracker. It is
         formal in discipline, not in logic. This chapter is the
         bridge: borge names properties and their rung; tools
         discharge them; status `verified` is reserved for
         machine-checked claims (see status-rungs).

         NON-GOALS (v1):
         - Prove the whole image, HTTP stack, or htmx UI
         - Prove LLM/agent behavior
         - Rewrite Nopales/Lisp into a verified language
         - Require proofs before ordinary feature work
         - Treat borge smoke stanzas as proofs
         - Revive Mirage/Solo5 as a verification dependency

         GOALS (v1 foothold):
         - Property registry lives in this chapter
         - QCheck suite exists under lisp/ for pure kernels
         - First three properties green (effect lattice,
           AST/reader round-trips, budget termination)
         - Clear path from QCheck → proof tools later
         - `verified` status means something real in reports
    |))
   )
  )

  (subsection status-rungs
   (doc "How property status maps onto borge status and tools")
   (status planned)
   (details
    (doc (|
         FORMALITY LADDER (climb per property, not per system):

         Rung 0 — Named
           Property written in this chapter with a stable id
           (e.g. effect.union-commutative). Still prose + intent.

         Rung 1 — Property-tested
           QCheck (or equivalent) quantifies over generated
           inputs. Counterexamples shrink and become named
           alcotest regressions. This is the default first
           implementation target.

         Rung 2 — Model-checked / exhaustive
           Finite domain fully enumerated, or a pure model
           checked against implementation oracles. Used for
           effect tags, small branch DAGs, auth state machines.

         Rung 3 — Proved
           Gospel/Cameleer, Why3, or Coq/Rocq discharge the
           property for the pure module. Reserved for small,
           stable kernels after Rung 1 is boring.

         BORGE STATUS MAPPING:
           planned      — property named, no suite yet
           in-progress  — generators/tests being written
           implemented  — property suite exists and is wired
                          into dune runtest (may still be weak)
           verified     — ONLY when the property's target rung
                          is green and owned:
                            * default target rung = 1 (QCheck)
                            * if a subsection claims proofs,
                              verified requires the proof artifact
           drifted      — suite fails, or code no longer matches
                          the stated property

         IMPORTANT:
         Existing product sections may stay `implemented` with
         smoke/alcotest verify stanzas. Do not mass-reclassify
         historical work. New FV work uses this chapter's rungs.
         Promoting an old section to `verified` requires an
         explicit property id from this chapter.
    |))
   )
  )

  (subsection scope-and-islands
   (doc "Verification islands in priority order")
   (status planned)
   (details
    (doc (|
         Island A — Pure Lisp kernel (FIRST)
           Packages: lisp/
           Modules: effect.ml, ast.ml, reader.ml, eval.ml, env.ml
           Why first: already pure, no Postgres/HTTP, small, high
           leverage under every product path.
           Starter properties: effect.*, reader.*, budget.*

         Island B — Auth password/session core (SECOND)
           Packages: image/lib/auth_password.ml (+ pure helpers)
           Properties: hash parse/serialize, verify correct/incorrect,
           no plaintext residual, later session allowlist logic.
           Keep HTTP handlers out of the proof boundary.

         Island C — Branch visibility model (THIRD)
           Extract a pure model of ancestry + binding visibility
           + merge conflict detection. Store/Postgres tests use
           the model as an oracle. Do not attempt live SQL proofs.

         Island D — Deferred
           http.ml routing tables (maybe model-check allowlists),
           transaction dynamic binding, package seed purity,
           Solo5/Mirage hardening. Not v1 FV scope.

         BOUNDARY RULE:
         Prefer pure functions with explicit results/errors.
         I/O wrappers call pure decision functions. Generators
         and proofs attach to the pure side only.
    |))
   )
  )

  (subsection tooling
   (doc "Tools, deps, and package layout for FV work")
   (status planned)
   (details
    (doc (|
         v1 TOOLING (normative for the foothold):
         - QCheck for property generation
         - qcheck-alcotest to wire into existing alcotest runners
         - dune runtest in lisp/ remains service-free
         - wall-clock timeouts around budget suites so a missed
           tick cannot hang CI forever

         LATER TOOLING (not required to start):
         - Gospel + Cameleer, or Why3 + Alt-Ergo/Z3, for Island A
           contracts once QCheck is boring
         - Coq/Rocq only for tiny kernels after properties stabilize
         - Custom exhaustive enumerators before heavy model checkers

         SUGGESTED LAYOUT:
           lisp/test/gen.ml
             shared generators (eff, expr, pure programs)
           lisp/test/properties_effect.ml
             Island A effect lattice properties
           lisp/test/properties_reader.ml
             AST JSON + surface round-trips
           lisp/test/properties_budget.ml
             budget termination / monotonicity
           docs/formal-verification.md  (optional human guide;
             this .borg chapter remains source of truth)

         DEPS:
         Add qcheck and qcheck-alcotest on the poohstack switch
         for lisp tests only. Do not pull proof tools into the
         image runtime closure.

         EQUALITY ORACLES (lock these defaults):
         - EffSet.t  → EffSet.to_list structural equality
         - expr      → Reader.pp_expr string equality until a
                       dedicated expr_equal exists
         - values    → Reader.pp_value for pure literals; never
                       require structural equality on closures
         - budget    → outcome tag only: Value | Budget | Error
    |))
   )
  )

  (subsection property-registry
   (doc "Named properties with stable ids; implement in registry order")
   (status planned)
   (details
    (doc (|
         Registry order is the default make order. Each property
         has: id, island, target rung, claim, notes.

         =========================================================
         ISLAND A — EFFECT LATTICE (implement first)
         =========================================================

         effect.union-commutative (rung 1)
           Claim: EffSet.union a b = EffSet.union b a

         effect.union-associative (rung 1)
           Claim: union (union a b) c = union a (union b c)

         effect.union-idempotent (rung 1)
           Claim: union a a = a

         effect.empty-identity (rung 1)
           Claim: union empty a = a AND union a empty = a

         effect.normalize-idempotent (rung 1)
           Claim: normalize (normalize xs) = normalize xs

         effect.pure-vanishes (rung 1)
           Claim: mem Pure (normalize xs) = false
           Notes: Pure is a label for empty capability, not a
           set member after normalize.

         effect.subset-union (rung 1)
           Claim: subset a (union a b) AND subset b (union a b)

         effect.policy-monotonic (rung 1)
           Claim: if subset allow1 allow2 and
                  policy_allows ~allow:allow1 got
                  then policy_allows ~allow:allow2 got

         =========================================================
         ISLAND A — READER / AST ROUND-TRIPS
         =========================================================

         reader.ast-json-roundtrip (rung 1)
           Claim: for generated depth-limited exprs without live
           closures, expr_of_json (expr_to_json e) yields e'
           with pp_expr e = pp_expr e'
           Notes: start here; cleaner than surface pretty-print.

         reader.surface-roundtrip (rung 1)
           Claim: for the same generator subset,
           read_one (pp_expr_flat e) is pp-equal to e
           (or equal after one normalization pass).
           Notes: allow printer canonicalization; do not force
           raw string identity if whitespace normalizes.

         reader.quote-reify-roundtrip (rung 1)
           Claim: for reader-surface exprs (no live closures),
           value_to_expr (quote_to_value e) is evaluation-
           equivalent for pure programs; prefer pp-equal where
           constructors align.
           Notes: matches lisp-interpreter macros round-trip
           invariant already written in that chapter.

         =========================================================
         ISLAND A — BUDGET / TERMINATION
         =========================================================

         budget.finite-outcome (rung 1)
           Claim: for pure closed terms and limit in a bounded
           range, eval finishes with Value | Interp_error |
           Budget_exceeded (no hang). Harness wall-clock guard
           required.

         budget.monotonic-success (rung 1)
           Claim: if e succeeds under limit N with value v, then
           for all M >= N it succeeds with the same pp_value.

         budget.divergence-trips (rung 1)
           Claim: known diverging shapes (self-app letrec, etc.)
           with small limit raise Budget_exceeded.
           Notes: keep hand examples + generated variants.

         =========================================================
         ISLAND A — EFFECT SOUNDNESS (after the above)
         =========================================================

         effect.pure-sound (rung 1, later rung 3 candidate)
           Claim: if Effect.infer e = Pure (under default prim
           lookup, no known_fn), then eval of e never invokes a
           non-pure prim.
           Notes: requires a test prim tracer or instrumented
           prim table. Highest-value semantic property once
           generators exist.

         =========================================================
         ISLAND B — AUTH (second wave; detail when started)
         =========================================================

         auth.password-roundtrip (rung 1)
           Claim: parse (format hash) preserves verify behavior.

         auth.verify-correct (rung 1)
           Claim: verify(password, hash(password, salt)) = true

         auth.verify-incorrect (rung 1)
           Claim: wrong password does not verify against hash.

         =========================================================
         ISLAND C — BRANCH MODEL (third wave; detail when started)
         =========================================================

         branch.visibility-parent (rung 1/2)
           Claim: child branch sees parent bindings unless
           overridden on the child.

         branch.merge-conflict-oracle (rung 1/2)
           Claim: pure merge model agrees with store conflict
           detection on generated small DAGs.
    |))
   )
  )

  (subsection phase-effect-lattice
   (doc "Pass A — QCheck laws for EffSet / policy")
   (status planned)
   (implements lisp/lib/effect.ml)
   (verify
    (test "lisp/test/properties_effect.ml — QCheck E1–E8 green under dune runtest")
    (build "cd lisp && dune build && dune runtest"))
   (details
    (doc (|
         FIRST IMPLEMENTATION PASS.

         Generators:
         - gen_eff: oneof all eff tags including Pure
         - gen_eff_list: list_size 0..6
         - gen_set: map EffSet.normalize gen_eff_list

         Implement properties:
           effect.union-commutative
           effect.union-associative
           effect.union-idempotent
           effect.empty-identity
           effect.normalize-idempotent
           effect.pure-vanishes
           effect.subset-union
           effect.policy-monotonic

         Wire via qcheck-alcotest into the lisp test runner.
         Keep existing effect_test.ml examples; do not delete them.
         Any shrunk counterexample becomes a named alcotest case.

         Done when: dune runtest in lisp/ is green and the eight
         properties are named in test output.
    |))
   )
  )

  (subsection phase-reader-roundtrip
   (doc "Pass B/C — AST JSON then surface syntax round-trips")
   (status planned)
   (implements lisp/lib/ast.ml lisp/lib/reader.ml lisp/lib/eval.ml)
   (verify
    (test "lisp/test/properties_reader.ml — JSON round-trip green")
    (test "optional surface + quote/reify properties green")
    (build "cd lisp && dune runtest"))
   (details
    (doc (|
         SECOND IMPLEMENTATION PASS.

         Generator discipline:
         - Generate ASTs, not arbitrary strings, first
         - Depth-bound (e.g. sized min n 3)
         - Start without macros, quasiquote, gensym, floats,
           with-transaction
         - Add constructors incrementally when green

         Order:
         1. reader.ast-json-roundtrip
         2. reader.surface-roundtrip (after JSON is boring)
         3. reader.quote-reify-roundtrip

         Equality: Reader.pp_expr unless expr_equal is added.
         Custom shrinkers that delete subexprs are encouraged
         once failures get noisy.
    |))
   )
  )

  (subsection phase-budget
   (doc "Pass D — budget termination and monotonicity")
   (status planned)
   (implements lisp/lib/eval.ml)
   (verify
    (test "lisp/test/properties_budget.ml — finite outcomes + divergence trips")
    (build "cd lisp && dune runtest")
    (smoke "budget suite has a wall-clock timeout guard"))
   (details
    (doc (|
         THIRD IMPLEMENTATION PASS.

         Harness:
         - pure prim table only
         - run eval under Lwt_main.run
         - catch Interp_error and classify Budget vs other
         - dune/alcotest timeout around the suite

         Properties:
           budget.finite-outcome
           budget.monotonic-success
           budget.divergence-trips

         Do not start this pass until effect + JSON round-trip
         generators are trustworthy enough to debug.
    |))
   )
  )

  (subsection phase-effect-soundness
   (doc "Pass E — infer Pure implies no non-pure prim dispatch")
   (status planned)
   (implements lisp/lib/effect.ml lisp/lib/eval.ml)
   (verify
    (test "instrumented prim tracer proves pure-sound on generated pure terms")
    (build "cd lisp && dune runtest"))
   (details
    (doc (|
         FOURTH IMPLEMENTATION PASS (still Island A).

         Technique:
         - Build prim table where each prim records its name
           into a trace cell, then delegates
         - Generate terms that Effect.infer marks Pure
         - Eval them; assert trace ⊆ pure_prims ∪ special forms

         This is the first deep semantic property and the best
         candidate to promote toward Gospel/Why3 later.
    |))
   )
  )

  (subsection phase-proofs
   (doc "Later: Gospel/Cameleer or Why3 on stable pure modules")
   (status planned)
   (details
    (doc (|
         Only after Passes A–E are boring.

         Preferred path for OCaml:
         1. Gospel specifications adjacent to effect.ml / pure helpers
         2. Cameleer or Why3 discharge lattice laws and pure-sound
            fragments
         3. Coq/Rocq only if a kernel is both critical and tiny

         Proof work must not block product Lisp packages.
         Runtime image must not depend on proof tooling.
    |))
   )
  )

  (subsection phase-auth-and-branch
   (doc "Second and third islands — auth then pure branch model")
   (status planned)
   (details
    (doc (|
         After Island A foothold:

         AUTH ISLAND:
         - Keep proofs/tests on auth_password and pure session
           helpers only
         - HTTP login handlers stay example/smoke tested
         - Properties: auth.password-roundtrip, verify-correct,
           verify-incorrect

         BRANCH ISLAND:
         - Extract pure visibility/merge model from store paths
         - Generate small branch DAGs
         - Oracle-compare model vs store behavior in tests
         - Properties: branch.visibility-parent,
           branch.merge-conflict-oracle

         Detail subsections may be expanded in a later plan pass
         when Island A is verified.
    |))
   )
  )

  (subsection workflow-integration
   (doc "How FV work uses borge plan/make/review")
   (status planned)
   (details
    (doc (|
         PLAN:
         - Edit this chapter (and only related .borg cross-links)
         - Name properties before writing tests when possible

         MAKE:
         - Implement generators + QCheck suites
         - If a property needs rewording, file `borge issue new`
           rather than editing this chapter mid-make

         REVIEW:
         - dune runtest green for touched packages
         - borge drift/lint/check clean enough for the change
         - Counterexamples promoted to named regressions

         COMMIT DISCIPLINE:
         - One property cluster per PR when practical
           (e.g. "QCheck EffSet lattice laws")
         - Do not mark verified until the subsection's verify
           stanza is honestly satisfied

         CROSS-CHAPTER LINKS:
         - lisp-interpreter.borg owns language semantics
         - this chapter owns how those semantics are checked
         - workflow.borg owns build/test commands; FV deps and
           runtest entrypoints should stay consistent with it
    |))
   )
  )

  (subsection success-criteria
   (doc "What 'FV foothold done' means for v1")
   (status planned)
   (verify
    (test "properties_effect + properties_reader JSON + properties_budget green")
    (build "dune runtest in lisp/")
    (smoke "borge report shows formal-verification subsections implemented/verified appropriately"))
   (details
    (doc (|
         v1 FOOTHOLD COMPLETE WHEN:
         1. qcheck wired for lisp tests
         2. effect lattice properties green (Pass A)
         3. AST JSON round-trip property green (Pass B)
         4. budget finite-outcome + divergence-trips green (Pass D)
         5. this chapter's phase-* subsections updated from
            planned → implemented (and verified where rung-1 holds)
         6. docs or agent-index mention how to run the property suite

         v1 FOOTHOLD EXPLICITLY DOES NOT REQUIRE:
         - Gospel/Why3 proofs
         - auth/branch islands
         - surface syntax perfection for all printers
         - zero flaky random seeds (pin counts; re-seed on fail)
    |))
   )
  )
 )
)
